\chapter{Games on a System Built for Other Work}
\label{ch:gaming}
\index{gaming on Linux}\index{Wine}\index{Proton}\index{Steam Deck}\index{Vulkan}

\section{The desktop needs applications people want}

An operating system can have excellent process isolation and networking
while remaining unattractive to someone whose important application does
not run. Games make this truth unusually visible. Their requirements span
graphics, sound, input, timing, content delivery, copy protection, and
social services. A missing piece can prevent play even when the kernel
and compiler work perfectly.

Unix had games from its earliest years, including the activities around
the PDP-7. Later Unix and Linux systems supported many native games and
ports. That continuity should not be confused with broad compatibility
with the commercial Windows game market. The latter required a much
larger body of APIs, middleware, drivers, and tested behavior.

Linux gaming therefore provides a useful test of the book's central
argument. A shared interface can make independently developed software
usable in a new environment, but the interface includes far more than
function names. Timing, undocumented assumptions, packaging, and failure
behavior can all become part of what compatibility must reproduce.

\section{Native ports still need an ecosystem}

A native port adapts a game's code and dependencies to the target platform.
Libraries such as SDL can abstract input, windows, and other services.
Cross-platform engines can reduce the work further. Yet an engine's
support does not guarantee that every plugin, codec, launcher, or online
service used by a particular game also supports the platform.

Developers must test different graphics drivers, audio configurations,
window systems, controllers, and library environments.
Distributing a binary raises questions about compatible runtime libraries
and supported distributions. A game that works on the developer's machine
may fail on a user's installation with a different loader or dependency.

Runtime containers and bundled libraries can reduce variation, but they
must cooperate with host components such as graphics drivers.
A program cannot always bundle its way out of the hardware boundary.
The installation environment and the machine's active driver stack still
form part of the effective platform.

The economics matter as well. A publisher compares expected audience,
support burden, testing cost, and opportunity cost. Technical feasibility
alone does not guarantee a port. A platform can consequently face a cycle:
fewer games reduce adoption, while a smaller audience reduces incentives
to support it.

\section{Wine implements a different application contract}

Wine provides a compatibility implementation for Windows applications on
Unix-like systems. It does not normally boot a full Windows guest operating
system for each application. It implements and translates the interfaces
applications expect, coordinating with the host's processes, files,
windowing, and other facilities.

That description needs a qualification. Running code for one processor
architecture on a different architecture can require additional translation
or emulation mechanisms. The claim that Wine is not a conventional
whole-machine emulator does not mean instruction-set differences vanish.
The application API and CPU execution model are separate layers.

Compatibility work includes observable behavior that a clean specification
may not capture. An application can depend on an undocumented return value,
a particular synchronization pattern, or a bug in a widely deployed
implementation. Reproducing that behavior can be necessary even when a
more elegant design would avoid it.

This makes compatibility engineering both empirical and historical.
Developers compare behavior, isolate regressions, and add targeted fixes.
The result can look inelegant locally while serving a coherent goal:
preserve an existing application's assumptions without requiring its
author to rewrite it.

\section{Proton assembles a gaming path}

Valve introduced Proton as part of its Steam Play work in 2018.
The official project overview describes it as a tool for running
Windows-exclusive games on Linux using Wine. Proton integrates and
coordinates additional components and fixes for the gaming environment;
it is not simply a new name for the Linux kernel or for all of Wine
\parencite{proton}.

DXVK translates major Direct3D 9, 10, and 11 interfaces toward Vulkan;
vkd3d-proton addresses Direct3D 12. These components are important because
modern games often express graphics work through Direct3D rather than a
native Linux API. Their translation path relies on capable Vulkan drivers
and appropriate hardware features.

Vulkan supplies a lower-level explicit graphics and compute API.
It does not automatically make translation perfect or costless.
Resource binding, synchronization, shader compilation, and feature support
must be mapped carefully. A game can be CPU-limited, GPU-limited,
or affected by synchronization overhead in ways that make simple
performance comparisons misleading.

Proton versions can change a game's behavior, and individual titles can
need specific fixes. The official README exposes configuration and logging
facilities, which themselves evolve. This book therefore explains the
architecture rather than promising that a particular title works under
every release. Compatibility is a tested relationship among a game,
a runtime, drivers, and hardware.

\section{A frame is a deadline, not just an image}
\index{frame pacing}\index{shader compilation}

A game must present useful frames at an acceptable cadence.
Average frame rate alone can hide stutter. A brief stall during shader
compilation or asset loading may be more noticeable than a modest
reduction in steady throughput. Frame pacing asks how evenly the work
arrives at the display.

The pipeline crosses many boundaries: input sampling, simulation,
command generation, driver processing, GPU execution, compositor policy,
and display scanout. Queueing can improve throughput while increasing
latency. Reducing a queue can improve responsiveness while making missed
deadlines more visible. The best configuration depends on hardware,
refresh behavior, and the application's goals.

Shader caches and precompilation can reduce repeated work, but cached
results depend on relevant versions and device properties.
A driver update can invalidate assumptions. A downloaded cache is not
a universal substitute for every shader a game might generate dynamically.
The practical success of gaming requires maintenance of these details,
not merely a functioning translation layer.

Audio and input have similar timing concerns. A controller must be
identified and mapped sensibly; focus changes and device reconnection
must behave predictably; suspend and resume must preserve a coherent
session. These are integration qualities that ordinary kernel benchmarks
rarely measure.

\section{Anti-cheat and protected services define another boundary}
\index{anti-cheat}

Some multiplayer games depend on anti-cheat systems and other protected
services. A compatibility layer may implement the game's ordinary APIs
while failing the service's requirements or authorization policy.
Some systems offer Linux or Proton support under particular conditions;
others depend on Windows-specific kernel mechanisms or publisher choices.

The obstacle is therefore not always a missing graphics function.
A server can refuse a client environment even when the game would otherwise
run. A publisher may need to enable a supported mode, validate it,
and accept its security tradeoffs. A universal statement that anti-cheat
``works'' or ``does not work'' on Linux conceals these distinctions.

This is another lesson about interfaces and institutions.
Compatibility includes permission to participate in a service.
Implementing a local behavior cannot compel a remote operator to accept
it. The effective platform boundary is partly technical and partly
organizational.

\section{The Steam Deck turns integration into a product}

The Steam Deck, released in 2022, made Linux gaming visible as a coherent
consumer device rather than solely a configurable desktop arrangement.
Its SteamOS environment combines a Linux base, graphics drivers,
compatibility tools, a controller-oriented interface, power management,
and a selected hardware platform.

The fixed hardware target reduces some variation without removing the
need for testing. A useful handheld experience requires readable text,
appropriate control prompts, workable launchers, acceptable performance,
and sensible suspend behavior. A game can render correctly yet remain
awkward on a small screen or require an unsupported input path.

The product illustrates how much value comes from curation and defaults.
The underlying components retain their separate identities, but users
benefit from an organization testing the combination.
This is related to distribution engineering and to the classic workstation
vendor's integration role, even though the market and licensing structure
are different.

Linux gaming's recent history thus returns to an old Unix problem:
how can independently developed parts form an environment people can
actually use? The answer includes stable interfaces, translation,
measurement, and extensive integration work. The same answer helps
explain how a Unix-like kernel reached an even larger audience through
phones.
